Carried patch, Windows only: a unit test reads its binary Gmsh mesh in binary mode.

"Electrostatic MMS analytic converges on curved tetrahedra" loads
test/data/mesh/cylinder-tet-p2.msh, a binary Gmsh 2.2 file, through a
std::ifstream opened in text mode. On Windows text mode turns CRLF into LF
and stops at 0x1A, so MFEM's reader got corrupted node records and aborted
with "Gmsh node indices are not unique". mfem/01 makes MFEM open its own
named files in binary mode, but this test hands MFEM the stream. The stream
now opens with std::ios::binary. Linux and macOS read the same bytes either
way.

diff --git a/test/unit/test-mms-electrostatic.cpp b/test/unit/test-mms-electrostatic.cpp
index 516c30d..c08b0f7 100644
--- a/test/unit/test-mms-electrostatic.cpp
+++ b/test/unit/test-mms-electrostatic.cpp
@@ -168,7 +168,7 @@ std::unique_ptr<mfem::Mesh> LoadCurvedCylinderMesh(int refinement)
 {
   const std::string mesh_path =
       std::string(PALACE_TEST_DATA_DIR) + "/mesh/cylinder-tet-p2.msh";
-  std::ifstream mesh_file(mesh_path);
+  std::ifstream mesh_file(mesh_path, std::ios::binary);
   REQUIRE(mesh_file.good());
   constexpr bool generate_edges = false, refine = true, fix_orientation = true;
   auto serial_mesh =
